---
// The bounded-numerics argument, drawn. This is the one picture the Proof page needs: a measured
// floor as a REGION rather than a line, and three readings that print differently depending on
// where they sit relative to it.
//
// DELIBERATELY A DIAGRAM, NOT A SCREENSHOT, and captioned as one. Every screenshot in the Proof
// repo (shots/, including shots/app-store/) renders the component tree without running a
// measurement, so all of them show an empty plot — "No rig chosen · nothing plotted yet". A shot
// of a blank grid teaches nothing and a shot of simulator output would caption a modelled number
// as a product claim. The numbers below are illustrative and the caption says so.
//
// Themed with CSS variables rather than the hardcoded hex the older diagrams use (see
// FrRegionsDiagram), so it follows the light/dark toggle like the rest of the page.
---

<figure class="floor-fig">
  <div class="figscroll">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 800 372" role="img"
         aria-label="A plot with the instrument's own noise floor drawn as a shaded region across frequency. Three readings are marked: one well above the floor prints as a number, one close to the floor prints as a range, and one below the floor prints as a less-than inequality.">

      <!-- dB grid -->
      <g class="grid">
        <line x1="60" y1="40" x2="740" y2="40"></line>
        <line x1="60" y1="121" x2="740" y2="121"></line>
        <line x1="60" y1="202" x2="740" y2="202"></line>
        <line x1="60" y1="284" x2="740" y2="284"></line>
        <line x1="60" y1="330" x2="740" y2="330" class="axis"></line>
      </g>
      <g class="tick" text-anchor="end">
        <text x="52" y="44">0</text>
        <text x="52" y="125">&minus;40</text>
        <text x="52" y="206">&minus;80</text>
        <text x="52" y="288">&minus;120</text>
      </g>

      <!-- The floor, as a shape. No converter has a flat one: it rises at both extremes, which is
           exactly why a single number for "the noise floor" is a fiction. -->
      <path class="floor-fill"
        d="M60 258 C140 272 210 283 300 286 C400 289 480 288 560 283 C640 278 700 268 740 260 L740 330 L60 330 Z"></path>
      <path class="floor-line"
        d="M60 258 C140 272 210 283 300 286 C400 289 480 288 560 283 C640 278 700 268 740 260"></path>

      <!-- Reading A — well clear of the floor. Prints as a number. -->
      <g class="rd real">
        <line x1="287" y1="202" x2="287" y2="286"></line>
        <circle cx="287" cy="202" r="5"></circle>
        <text x="287" y="188" text-anchor="middle">&minus;78.4 dB</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Reading B — close to the floor. Prints as a RANGE, never a plus-or-minus: a floor can
           only push a reading up, so the uncertainty is one-sided.
           Drawn as a capped range bar rather than a marker. The first version was a small rect and
           read as a rendering glitch at page size — the whole point of this reading is that it has
           EXTENT, so the extent has to be the thing you see. -->
      <g class="marginal">
        <line class="bar" x1="445" y1="264" x2="445" y2="286"></line>
        <line class="cap" x1="437" y1="264" x2="453" y2="264"></line>
        <line class="cap" x1="437" y1="286" x2="453" y2="286"></line>
        <text x="445" y="254" text-anchor="middle">&minus;119 … &minus;108 dB</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Reading C — under the floor. Prints as an inequality, because that is the honest answer:
           the floor is all that is known. The hollow marker sits ON the floor, not below it: the
           floor is the only value there is to draw. -->
      <g class="bound">
        <line x1="621" y1="281" x2="621" y2="300" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line>
        <circle cx="621" cy="281" r="5" class="hollow"></circle>
        <text x="621" y="314" text-anchor="middle">&lt; &minus;116 dB</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Frequency axis -->
      <g class="tick" text-anchor="middle">
        <text x="60" y="348">20</text>
        <text x="218" y="348">100</text>
        <text x="445" y="348">1k</text>
        <text x="672" y="348">10k</text>
        <text x="740" y="348">20k</text>
      </g>

      <text class="floorlabel" x="150" y="308">THE INSTRUMENT&rsquo;S OWN FLOOR</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    <b>Three readings, three different printings.</b> Clear of the floor, a reading is a number.
    Close to it, a range &mdash; never a plus-or-minus, because a floor can only push a
    reading <i>up</i>. Under it, an inequality, because the floor is all that is known.
    <span class="fig-note">A diagram. The values are illustrative, not a measurement of anything.</span>
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .floor-fig {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 20px 20px 18px;
    /* THE FIGURE IS A GRID ITEM AND THE SVG BELOW HAS A min-width. Without this, the item's default
       min-width:auto refuses to shrink under the svg's min-content width, .figscroll never gets to
       scroll, and the PAGE scrolls sideways instead — measured at 236px of horizontal overflow on a
       390pt phone before this line existed. Any wide child inside a grid or flex item needs the
       same treatment; the overflow container alone is not enough. */
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .figscroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .floor-fig svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 560px;
    height: auto;
  }

  .grid line {
    stroke: var(--line);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .grid line.axis {
    stroke: var(--line-soft);
  }
  .tick text {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    fill: var(--muted-2);
  }

  /* The floor is a region, not a line — the whole point. */
  .floor-fill {
    fill: var(--muted);
    opacity: 0.16;
  }
  .floor-line {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--muted);
    stroke-width: 1.4;
    stroke-dasharray: 5 3;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  .floorlabel {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 9.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    fill: var(--muted-2);
  }

  .rd text,
  .marginal text,
  .bound text {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    fill: var(--text);
  }
  .rd line {
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }

  /* Accent is reserved for signal state site-wide, and "this reading is real" is exactly that. */
  .real circle {
    fill: var(--accent);
  }
  .real line {
    stroke: var(--accent);
  }
  .real text {
    fill: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The range bar. Full-strength ink, because a range IS the reading here — not an annotation on
     one. */
  .marginal .bar {
    stroke: var(--text);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
  }
  .marginal .cap {
    stroke: var(--text);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
  }

  .bound circle.hollow {
    fill: var(--bg);
    stroke: var(--muted);
    stroke-width: 1.4;
  }
  .bound line {
    stroke: var(--muted);
  }
  .bound text {
    fill: var(--muted);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  figcaption b {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .fig-note {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--muted-2);
  }
</style>
